      },
      {
        "id": "msg-1787033350878-assistant",
        "conversationId": "conv-1787033346147",
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "### Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) Overview\n\nReciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) is an algorithmic method used to merge ranked lists generated by disparate retrieval mechanisms [[01]]. \n\n#### Core Mechanics\n*   **Score Calculation:** RRF computes a score by summing the reciprocal of the rank for each candidate across different sets (e.g., vector and BM25) [[01]].\n*   **Formula:** The score is defined as $\\sum (1 / (k + \\text{rank}_i))$, where $k$ is a constant parameter set to 60 [[01]].\n*   **Calibration:** The algorithm does not require score calibration between the different retrieval systems being merged [[01]].\n\n#### Role in Hybrid Pipelines\n*   **Complementarity:** RRF is utilized in hybrid search pipelines to combine the strengths of dense semantic embeddings with BM25 [[01]], [[02]].\n*   **Retrieval Balance:** While dense embeddings focus on semantic meaning, BM25 provides essential recall for exact keyword matches, code identifiers, acronyms, and alphanumeric part numbers where dense models may struggle [[02]].",
        "createdAt": "2026-08-18T06:09:10.878Z",
        "citations": [
          {
            "id": "cit-chk-1-3-1",
            "citationIndex": 1,
            "documentId": "doc-1",
            "documentTitle": "Dense Retrieval and RAG Architecture Specification.pdf",
            "sourceType": "PDF",
            "pageNumber": 8,
            "section": "3. Reciprocal Rank Fusion Algorithm",
            "chunkId": "chk-1-3",
            "excerpt": "Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) algorithmically merges ranked lists from disparate retrieval mechanisms without requiring score calibration. Given parameter k=60, RRF score = sum(1 / (k + rank_i)) across vector and BM25 candidate sets.",
            "addedDate": "8/18/2026",
            "score": 0.51
          },
          {
            "id": "cit-chk-3-2-2",
